{:edb/db-before-t 1200 :edb/db-after-t 1201 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534513 50 #inst "2026-10-12T01:33:03.416Z" 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186161378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161379 13194139534513 17592186045417 false] [17592186161378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161380 13194139534513 17592186045417 false] [17592186161378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161381 13194139534513 17592186045417 false] [17592186161378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161382 13194139534513 17592186045417 false] [17592186161378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161383 13194139534513 17592186045417 false] [17592186161378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423869 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186161378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423870 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186161378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423871 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186161378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423877 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186161378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423881 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186161378 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423877 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186161817 1071 "The discrete random variable $X\\in \\mathbb Z$ can be approximated by the continuous random variable $Y$. By applying an appropriate continuity correction, $P(X = -1)$ can be approximated as $P(a\\leq Y \\leq b)$. Find the values of $a$ and $b$." 13194139534513 17592186045417 false] [17592186161817 1071 "The discrete random variable $X\\in \\mathbb Z$ can be approximated by the continuous random variable $Y.$ By applying an appropriate continuity correction, $P(X = -1)$ can be approximated as $P(a\\leq Y \\leq b).$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$" 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186161817 1074 "We construct an interval around $X=-1$ with a radius of $0.5{:}$\n\n![](images/e9/e92a0fb229125f6a9cd27c96629cd1851f3356ece0bca6aa77b8393f7a0dfd90.png)\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\nP(X =-1) \\mid \\approx P(-1 - 0.5 \\le Y \\le - 1 + 0.5); = P(-1.5 \\le Y \\le - 0.5)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-1.5$ and $b=-0.5$." 13194139534513 17592186045417 false] [17592186161817 1074 "We construct an interval around $X=-1$ with a radius of $0.5{:}$\n\n![](images/e9/e92a0fb229125f6a9cd27c96629cd1851f3356ece0bca6aa77b8393f7a0dfd90.png)\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=-1) & ≈P(-1-0.5\\le Y\\le -1+0.5) \\\\  & =P(-1.5\\le Y\\le -0.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-1.5$ and $b=-0.5.$" 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186161818 1071 "The discrete random variable $X\\in \\mathbb  Z$ can be approximated by the continuous random variable $Y.$ By applying an appropriate continuity correction, $P(-1 \\lt X \\lt 2)$ can be approximated as $P(a\\leq Y \\leq b).$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$" 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186161818 1071 "The discrete random variable $X\\in \\mathbb Z$ can be approximated by the continuous random variable $Y$. By applying an appropriate continuity correction, $P(-1 \\lt X \\lt 2)$ can be approximated as $P(a\\leq Y \\leq b)$. Find the values of $a$ and $b$." 13194139534513 17592186045417 false] [17592186161818 1074 "To apply the appropriate continuity correction, we first rewrite the strict inequalities using non-strict inequalities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-1 < X < 2) &= P(0 \\le X \\le 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we extend the endpoints of the interval\n\n$$\n0 \\leq X \\leq 1\n$$\n\nby $0.5$. This gives\n\n$$\nP(0 \\le X \\le 1) \\mid \\approx P(0 - 0.5 \\le Y \\le 1 + 0.5); = P(-0.5 \\le Y \\le 1.5)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-0.5$ and $b=1.5$.\n\n![](images/52/52e5ddb2af86cba41ad9b05001e6a8720b973492f4cf5239031b972c5bcf2afb.png)" 13194139534513 17592186045417 false] [17592186161818 1074 "To apply the appropriate continuity correction, we first rewrite the strict inequalities using non-strict inequalities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<X<2) & =P(0\\le X\\le 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we extend the endpoints of the interval $0 \\leq X \\leq 1$ by $0.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 1) & ≈P(0-0.5\\le Y\\le 1+0.5) \\\\  & =P(-0.5\\le Y\\le 1.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-0.5$ and $b=1.5.$\n\n![](images/52/52e5ddb2af86cba41ad9b05001e6a8720b973492f4cf5239031b972c5bcf2afb.png)" 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186161819 1071 "The discrete random variable $X\\in \\mathbb Z$ can be approximated by the continuous random variable $Y$. By applying an appropriate continuity correction, $P(X > 5)$ can be approximated as $P(Y\\geq a)$. Find the value of $a$." 13194139534513 17592186045417 false] [17592186161819 1071 "The discrete random variable $X\\in \\mathbb Z$ can be approximated by the continuous random variable $Y.$ By applying an appropriate continuity correction, $P(X > 5)$ can be approximated as $P(Y\\geq a).$ Find the value of $a.$" 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186161819 1074 "To apply the appropriate continuity correction, we first rewrite the strict inequalities using non-strict inequalities, as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X > 5) =P(X \\geq 6)\n$$\n\nThen, we extend the endpoints of the interval\n\n$$\nX \\geq 6\n$$\n\nby $0.5$. This gives\n\n$$\nP(X \\ge 6) \\mid \\approx P(Y \\ge 6 - 0.5); = P(Y \\ge 5.5)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=5.5$.\n\n![](images/4b/4b6c945ad08ff50b4d306e1952947cb55d5a05899e91f254f3dde3edde9f6523.png)" 13194139534513 17592186045417 false] [17592186161819 1074 "To apply the appropriate continuity correction, we first rewrite the strict inequalities using non-strict inequalities, as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X > 5) =P(X \\geq 6)\n$$\n\nThen, we extend the endpoints of the interval $X \\geq 6$ by $0.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & ≈P(Y\\ge 6-0.5) \\\\  & =P(Y\\ge 5.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=5.5.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b6c945ad08ff50b4d306e1952947cb55d5a05899e91f254f3dde3edde9f6523.png)" 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186162025 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423901 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186162025 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423902 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186162026 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423903 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186162026 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423904 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186162027 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423905 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186162160 1050 420.0 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186162681 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186162681 1071 "The discrete random variable $X ∈ Z$ can be approximated by the continuous random variable $Y$. By applying an appropriate continuity correction, $P(X > 2)$ can be approximated as" 13194139534513 17592186045417 false] [17592186162681 1071 "The discrete random variable $X\\in Z$ can be approximated by the continuous random variable $Y.$ By applying an appropriate continuity correction, $P(X>2)$ can be approximated as" 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186162681 1074 "To apply the appropriate continuity correction, we first rewrite the strict inequality as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X>2)=P(X\\ge 3)\n$$\n\nThen, we extend the endpoints of the interval $X\\ge 3$ by $0.5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & ≈P(Y\\ge 3-0.5) \\\\  & =P(Y\\ge 2.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186163245 1064 "We often want to approximate a discrete random variable $X$ by a continuous random variable $Y$. To do this, we need to consider how to treat the values of $Y$ that lie between the discrete values of $X$.\n\nFirst, recall that if $X = 2$ *to the nearest integer*, then\n\n$$\n1.5\\leq X \\lt 2.5\n$$\n\nNow consider the following scenario:\n\n- $X\\in\\big\\{\\ldots, 0,1,2, 3\\ldots\\big\\}$ is a *discrete* random variable with *integer* support\n- $Y\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$ is a *continuous* random variable that we will use to approximate $X$\n\nTo approximate the probability\n\n$$\nP(X = 2)\n$$\n\nusing the random variable $Y$, we need to calculate\n\n$$\nP(1.5 \\leq Y \\lt 2.5)\n$$\n\nAlso, since $Y$ is continuous, this probability is equal to\n\n$$\nP(1.5 \\leq Y \\leq 2.5)\n$$\n\nWe can think of the required values of $Y$ as lying within the interval contained within a circle of radius $0.5$ centered at $Y=2$.\n\n![](images/8a/8af3b4a73cdf37169e0841ce7ef9bbe31fefd171cdeacc33dcc8cedca0facfe8.png)\n\nAdjusting the values of a discrete random variable so that it can be approximated using a continuous random variable is called a **continuity correction.**" 13194139534513 17592186045417 false] [17592186163245 1064 "We often want to approximate a discrete random variable $X$ by a continuous random variable $Y.$ To do this, we need to consider how to treat the values of $Y$ that lie between the discrete values of $X.$\n\nFirst, recall that if $X = 2$ *to the nearest integer*, then\n\n$$\n1.5\\leq X \\lt 2.5.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following scenario:\n\n- $X\\in\\big\\{\\ldots, 0,1,2, 3\\ldots\\big\\}$ is a *discrete* random variable with *integer* support\n\n- $Y\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$ is a *continuous* random variable that we will use to approximate $X$\n\nTo approximate the probability\n\n$$\nP(X = 2)\n$$\n\nusing the random variable $Y,$ we need to calculate\n\n$$\nP( 1.5 \\leq Y \\lt 2.5).\n$$\n\nAlso, since $Y$ is continuous, this probability is equal to\n\n$$\nP( 1.5 \\leq Y \\leq 2.5).\n$$\n\nWe can think of the required values of $Y$ as lying within the interval contained within a circle of radius $0.5$ centered at $Y=2.$\n\n![](images/8a/8af3b4a73cdf37169e0841ce7ef9bbe31fefd171cdeacc33dcc8cedca0facfe8.png)\n\nAdjusting the values of a discrete random variable so that it can be approximated using a continuous random variable is called a **continuity correction.**" 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186423866 1080 "P(Y\\ge 20)" 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186423866 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186423866 1083 #uuid "055ee122-3724-57f6-b149-fab95924d791" 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186423867 1080 "P(Y\\ge 19)" 13194139534513 17592186045417 true] [17592186423867 108